Here's everything — the two I mentioned before, the other useful repos from the web, and the reels.
The "other one" I mentioned
browser-use/browser-use — 119,485★, MIT — the biggest open-source AI browser agent. pip install browser-use, then you describe the task in plain English and the LLM drives a real browser via Playwright (DOM + screenshots). Supports local models via Ollama, which matters on your org laptop. Caveat: it needs an LLM API key (or a local model) and it's non-deterministic — great for exploration, less so for repeatable runs.
browserbase/stagehand — 25,859★ — SDK for extracting data and interacting with any site; its own docs say "get started with Claude Code." More of a developer toolkit than a ready agent.
Other repos worth knowing (web search)
agentrhq/webcmd — 2,753★, Apache-2.0 — this is your idea as a product: a self-learning browser. The agent learns a website once (pages, states, actions, workflows, pitfalls) and stores it as a local agent-facing sitemap, then compiles it into deterministic per-site CLI commands. Scored 67/100 on the BU Bench V1 benchmark at $0.255/task — cheapest of the five tools tested. Launched Oct 9, 2026 by AgentR.
pinchtab/pinchtab — 10,471★, MIT — local-first, single ~30MB Go binary, zero telemetry, binds to 127.0.0.1. Claude drives it through bash with no MCP: pinchtab nav <url>, pinchtab snap -i, pinchtab click e5, pinchtab fill e3 "text". Token-efficient (~800 tokens vs 10,000 for raw HTML) and persistent profiles keep you logged in — big for SSO. Very org-laptop friendly.
